PUBLIC RESPONSE: {B

The Sale of Goods Act (Ontario) s. 19 Rule 2 is the controlling provision here. The MacBook was specific goods that required the seller to complete repairs before they reached a deliverable state, so risk remained with Jake until two conditions were met: completion of the work and notice to the buyer that the goods were then in that state. Jake finished the screen replacement and, on 5 June, gave Luke explicit notice that the laptop would be ready for pickup the following day. That message supplied both the fact of completion and a precise readiness date, satisfying the statutory notice requirement. Once those steps occurred, property and risk passed to Luke, and the later flood loss fell on him.  

Colleague 12’s reading of the June 5 text as insufficient because it used the phrase “almost finished” is too narrow. The message as a whole conveyed that the repair would be done by the stated pickup date, which is the functional equivalent of notice that the goods would be deliverable. The Act does not demand any particular formality or wording beyond conveying that the work is complete and the goods are ready; a text specifying an exact date meets that threshold. Accordingly, Jake is not required to refund the price, and B remains the accurate statement of the outcome.}

